patch of cells, "}]},{"bbox":[56.519439697265625,227.8822021484375,541.0780639648438,240.79495239257812],"spans":[{"text":"including nematocysts, at the distal end of the upper side of the bract is very distinctive. Its bracts and those of "},{"text":"E. "}]},{"bbox":[56.519439697265625,241.3226318359375,541.1288452148438,254.33340454101562],"spans":[{"text":"insidiator"},{"text":" "},{"text":"sp. nov. "},{"text":"are distinctly larger than for the others. For the others, the half transverse fold across the middle "}]},{"bbox":[56.518394470214844,254.88311767578125,541.1112670898438,267.7958679199219],"spans":[{"text":"of the bract distinguishes "},{"text":"E. laciniata"},{"text":", while the others are distinguished by their relative proportions and the "}]},{"bbox":[56.51734924316406,270.7983093261719,188.05746459960938,281.2352600097656],"spans":[{"text":"positioning of the lateral cusps."}]},{"bbox":[74.5168685913086,281.88299560546875,541.1676635742188,294.7957458496094],"spans":[{"text":"The tentilla of the various "},{"text":"Erenna"},{"text":" species are compared in Figure 51, and each should be easily distinguishable "}]},{"bbox":[56.51734924316406,295.3223876953125,541.0811157226562,308.2351379394531],"spans":[{"text":"when closely examined. They are also a useful guide for "},{"text":"in situ"},{"text":" identification. The small size of those of "},{"text":"Erenna "}]},{"bbox":[56.517333984375,308.88287353515625,541.2100830078125,321.7956237792969],"spans":[{"text":"laciniata"},{"text":" give the extended tentacle a beaded appearance (see Figure 12), and the forked Y-shaped base of the "}]},{"bbox":[56.517337799072266,322.3233337402344,541.0759887695312,335.236083984375],"spans":[{"text":"cnidoband of the tentillum is readily seen. In comparison, the often densely packed tentilla of "},{"text":"E. richardi"},{"text":" are larger "}]},{"bbox":[56.518402099609375,335.88385009765625,541.1290893554688,348.7966003417969],"spans":[{"text":"and have a spiky appearance (see Figure 1). Despite "},{"text":"E. cornuta"},{"text":" having three types of characteristic tentilla, the "}]},{"bbox":[56.5184326171875,349.3243103027344,541.1574096679688,362.237060546875],"spans":[{"text":"colonies are best identified "},{"text":"in situ"},{"text":" by the long patches of cells at the distal ends of the bracts. Although the tentillum "}]},{"bbox":[56.51739501953125,362.88482666015625,541.0948486328125,375.8955993652344],"spans":[{"text":"of "},{"text":"E. sirena"},{"text":" "},{"text":"sp. nov."},{"text":" bears a very distinctive red lure, it is often difficult to see in the "},{"text":"in situ"},{"text":" photographs and it is "}]},{"bbox":[56.517425537109375,378.80108642578125,541.1947021484375,389.238037109375],"spans":[{"text":"often easier to deduce the identity of the colony by the absence of the characters of the other species. Nonetheless, "}]},{"bbox":[56.517425537109375,389.88580322265625,541.1115112304688,402.7985534667969],"spans":[{"text":"the species has acquired the nickname of the \"pompom "},{"text":"Erenna"},{"text":"\" as a result of its ability to extend the base of the "}]},{"bbox":[56.518463134765625,405.80206298828125,541.201171875,416.239013671875],"spans":[{"text":"tentacle so that cluster of tentilla are positioned well away from the siphosomal stem (see Figure 41). It appears that "}]},{"bbox":[56.518463134765625,416.88677978515625,541.1533203125,429.7995300292969],"spans":[{"text":"E. laciniata"},{"text":" also has this ability, but not to such a great extent (see Figure 12). However, as with the red lures, this "}]},{"bbox":[56.518463134765625,430.3272399902344,541.1187744140625,443.3380126953125],"spans":[{"text":"feature is not to be seen on every colony observed.  Finally "},{"text":"E. insidiator "},{"text":"sp. nov."},{"text":" tentilla can clearly be identified "}]},{"bbox":[56.5184326171875,446.3635559082031,480.9357604980469,456.8005065917969],"spans":[{"text":"from the long wing-like cnidoband that is very prominent on the small specimen shown in Figure 38."}]},{"bbox":[56.52000045776367,673.7591552734375,540.836181640625,683.333740234375],"spans":[{"text":"FIGURE 51. "},{"text":"Comparison of the tentilla of "},{"text":"Erenna"},{"text":" species. Scale bars: 1 mm for "},{"text":"E. laciniata, E. cornuta & E. sirena"},{"text":" "},{"text":"sp. nov"},{"text":".; "}]},{"bbox":[56.520965576171875,685.2792358398438,227.75840759277344,694.8538208007812],"spans":[{"text":"5 mm for "},{"text":"E. richardi"},{"text":" & "},{"text":"E. insidiator"},{"text":" "},{"text":"sp. nov"},{"text":"."}]},{"bbox":[74.5199966430664,710.2384033203125,541.160400390625,720.6753540039062],"spans":[{"text":"Finally, the depth distribution for each species in the vicinity of Monterey Bay and the Gulf of Mexico are "}]},{"bbox":[56.520477294921875,721.3231811523438,541.10498046875,734.3339233398438],"spans":[{"text":"compared in Figure 52. The two known colonies of "},{"text":"Erenna insidiator"},{"text":" "},{"text":"sp. nov."},{"text":" both occurred in Monterey Bay and "}]},{"bbox":[56.52044677734375,734.7636108398438,541.0958251953125,747.7743530273438],"spans":[{"text":"at depths (< 2800 m) distinctly deeper than any other "},{"text":"Erenna"},{"text":" species. "},{"text":"E. sirena"},{"text":" "},{"text":"sp. nov."},{"text":" also occurred there over a "}]},{"bbox":[56.52044677734375,750.7999267578125,541.173583984375,761.2368774414062],"spans":[{"text":"quite separate depth range that did not overlap with any other (i.e. c. 1900-2450 m). For the other three species the "}]}]}}}
